WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.990
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.595
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:16.354
Unit twenty-five: Sociolinguistics. Today you will describe typical linguistic behaviour with 'tend to' and 'be inclined to'.

4
00:00:16.654 --> 00:00:19.190
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:23.146
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:24.845
Dialect.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.359
Dialect means a regional or social variety of a language.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.052
Battambang speakers use some distinctive dialect words.

9
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.826
Your turn. Say: Dialect.

10
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:39.552
Dialect.

11
00:00:40.317 --> 00:00:41.271
Accent.

12
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:44.608
Accent means a way of pronouncing a language.

13
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:47.936
Her accent reveals that she grew up abroad.

14
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.924
Your turn. Say: Accent.

15
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:54.872
Accent.

16
00:00:55.617 --> 00:00:56.745
Code-switching.

17
00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.130
Code-switching means moving between two languages or varieties in conversation.

18
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:06.167
Students often code-switch between Khmer and English.

19
00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:09.726
Your turn. Say: Code-switching.

20
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.195
Code-switching.

21
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:15.986
Identity.

22
00:01:16.983 --> 00:01:20.283
Identity means the sense of who a person or group is.

23
00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:23.452
Language is closely tied to identity.

24
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.668
Your turn. Say: Identity.

25
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:30.534
Identity.

26
00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:32.261
Prestige.

27
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.550
Prestige means high status or respect given to something.

28
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.090
English carries prestige in many job markets.

29
00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:43.463
Your turn. Say: Prestige.

30
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.243
Prestige.

31
00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:49.089
Variation.

32
00:01:50.083 --> 00:01:53.186
Variation means differences in the way language is used.

33
00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:56.561
Variation exists even within one family.

34
00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.582
Your turn. Say: Variation.

35
00:02:02.433 --> 00:02:03.446
Variation.

36
00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:05.254
Community.

37
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.364
Community means a group sharing a place or interests.

38
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.832
Each online community develops its own slang.

39
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:16.429
Your turn. Say: Community.

40
00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:20.384
Community.

41
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.093
Register.

42
00:02:23.083 --> 00:02:26.836
Register means language variety used for a particular purpose.

43
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.506
Students switch to a formal register in essays.

44
00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:33.328
Your turn. Say: Register.

45
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:37.005
Register.

46
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.532
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:48.391
Visal, your survey looked at how students speak in different situations. What was the clearest pattern?

48
00:02:49.483 --> 00:02:59.334
Speakers tend to code-switch in informal settings. With friends, students mix Khmer and English freely, often in the same sentence.

49
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.357
And in formal situations?

50
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:13.521
They are much less inclined to switch. In class presentations or with elders, they generally keep to one language and a formal register.

51
00:03:14.617 --> 00:03:16.489
Why do they code-switch at all?

52
00:03:17.583 --> 00:03:25.460
Partly for identity. English words tend to signal modernity and prestige, while Khmer expresses closeness and respect.

53
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:27.996
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:32.382
Grammar focus: Tend to and be inclined to.

55
00:03:32.682 --> 00:03:33.857
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:38.245
Speakers tend to code-switch in informal settings.

57
00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.508
Students are less inclined to switch in formal situations.

58
00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:47.018
English words tend to signal modernity and prestige.

59
00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:57.797
'Tend to' and 'be inclined to' describe general patterns without claiming that something is always true. They are useful hedges in social science. Both are followed by the base verb.

60
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.921
Be careful! Don't say: Speakers tend code-switching at home.

61
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:07.749
Say: Speakers tend to code-switch at home.

62
00:04:08.049 --> 00:04:10.153
Use 'tend to' + base verb.

63
00:04:10.967 --> 00:04:13.979
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:16.244
Language and Society.

65
00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:22.885
Sociolinguistics studies how language varies according to region, class, age and social situation.

66
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.651
Speakers tend to adjust their accent and vocabulary depending on whom they are addressing.

67
00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:36.264
In multilingual communities, people are often inclined to code-switch, which can signal solidarity or identity.

68
00:04:37.267 --> 00:04:44.177
Varieties associated with powerful groups usually acquire prestige, whereas others may be unfairly stigmatised.

69
00:04:45.167 --> 00:04:50.858
Sociolinguists therefore argue that no dialect is inherently better than another, only different.

70
00:04:51.967 --> 00:04:56.061
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:56.917 --> 00:04:59.334
Teenagers blank use more slang than adults.

72
00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:03.009
A, tend to. B, tend. C, are tending.

73
00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:08.276
The answer is A: tend to.

74
00:05:09.417 --> 00:05:12.113
She is inclined blank formal language at work.

75
00:05:12.413 --> 00:05:15.602
A, using. B, to use. C, use.

76
00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:21.213
The answer is B: to use.

77
00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.441
Moving between languages in one conversation is blank.

78
00:05:25.741 --> 00:05:29.835
A, code-switching. B, prestige. C, dialect.

79
00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.771
The answer is A: code-switching.

80
00:05:36.917 --> 00:05:39.915
High status given to a language variety is blank.

81
00:05:40.215 --> 00:05:44.007
A, variation. B, community. C, prestige.

82
00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:49.938
The answer is C: prestige.

83
00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:55.219
According to the reading, sociolinguists believe that no dialect is…

84
00:05:55.519 --> 00:06:01.100
A, inherently better than another. B, worth studying. C, used in communities.

85
00:06:04.633 --> 00:06:07.852
The answer is A: inherently better than another.

86
00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:12.334
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.183 --> 00:06:16.170
When do you switch between languages or styles of speaking?

88
00:06:32.983 --> 00:06:34.913
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:35.213 --> 00:07:08.849
I tend to switch between languages most when I am chatting with friends who also study English. We are inclined to use English words for technology or school subjects, while Khmer feels more natural for jokes and emotions. By contrast, when I speak with my grandparents, I keep to polite Khmer and a respectful register. Some people believe code-switching shows poor language skills. I disagree, because choosing the right style for each situation actually requires considerable skill.

90
00:07:09.149 --> 00:07:11.102
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:12.017 --> 00:07:16.561
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and tend to and be inclined to.

92
00:07:16.861 --> 00:07:22.114
Today's value is respect for differences. Everyone is different, and everyone is special.

93
00:07:22.414 --> 00:07:25.531
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:25.831 --> 00:07:28.658
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

